    { text: 'Lally, P. et al. (2010). How are habits formed: modelling habit formation in the real world. EJSP, 40(6), 998–1009.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674' },
    { text: 'Wood, W., & Neal, D. T. (2007). A new look at habits and the habit-goal interface. Psychological Review, 114(4), 843–863.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.114.4.843' },
    { text: 'Zimmerman, B. J. (2002). Becoming a self-regulated learner: an overview. Theory Into Practice, 41(2), 64–70.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1207/s15430421tip4102_2' },
  ]}
>
  <p>Motivation is a bad manager. It shows up when things are easy and vanishes when they are hard. A routine is what keeps you working on the days motivation calls in sick, because the decision has already been made.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why routines beat willpower</h2>
  <p>Every time you decide whether to study, you spend energy and risk saying no. Wood and Neal's research on habits shows that behaviours tied to a stable context run almost on autopilot, bypassing that daily negotiation. A routine converts "should I study now?" into "it is 6pm, so I study", which is far cheaper to obey.</p>

  <h2 id="build">Build yours in four steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Anchor it.</strong> Attach study to something you already do daily: after your last class, after dinner. The anchor is the trigger.</li>
    <li><strong>Start tiny.</strong> One 25-minute block is enough to begin. You can grow it once it is automatic.</li>
    <li><strong>Name the task.</strong> Decide in advance what each block is for, using the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the loop.</strong> End with a tick, a note of what you did, and tomorrow's first task ready.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="sample">A sample daily routine</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">Block</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>After last class</td><td>10 min: review today\u2019s notes, log homework</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Early evening</td><td>Block 1 (25/5): hardest subject or nearest deadline</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evening</td><td>Block 2 (25/5): second subject or flashcards</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Before bed</td><td>5 min: tick off, set tomorrow\u2019s first task</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Lay your version into the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a> and run each block with the <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro timer</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="stick">Make it stick</h2>
  <p>Expect weeks before it feels automatic; Lally found a median of about two months. Track a simple streak, keep the "never miss twice" rule, and resist piling on more the moment it feels easy. For the right amount of daily work, see <a href="/guides/how-many-hours-to-study-a-day/">how many hours to study a day</a>, and for timing it to your energy, <a href="/guides/best-time-to-study/">the best time to study</a>.</p>
</Guide>
